# Independently authored sentinel slice for the GPL
# MTR-format runner.  Combines two unrelated PG/SQL-
# standard surfaces that both parse-reject today, so a
# single slice locks down both gaps with one runner
# invocation.  NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/ or PG's regress.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - UNION CORRESPONDING (bare, no BY): pinned at one
#     probe in set_ops_edges_basic_extra:111. That probe
#     covers only the bare UNION shape.
#   - INTERSECT CORRESPONDING (bare or BY): ZERO prior
#     coverage. Grep'd tests/main/*.test for the literal
#     substrings "INTERSECT.*CORRESP", "EXCEPT.*CORRESP",
#     "CORRESPONDING BY" — only the one UNION hit above.
#   - EXCEPT CORRESPONDING (bare or BY): ZERO prior
#     coverage.
#   - UNION CORRESPONDING BY (cols): ZERO prior coverage
#     (the existing probe is bare, no BY clause).
#   - ALTER SCHEMA (any shape): ZERO prior coverage.
#     Grep'd tests/main/*.test for "ALTER SCHEMA" — no
#     hits.
#
# This slice pins:
#
# A. The SQL-standard set-op CORRESPONDING family on the
#    two set-op verbs not yet pinned (INTERSECT, EXCEPT)
#    plus the BY (cols) form on all three verbs.  All
#    five shapes share the same sqlparser-rs parse-
#    reject fingerprint with the column index landing
#    exactly at the CORRESPONDING token, which is
#    determined by the prefix length and the set-op
#    keyword length:
#      - INTERSECT (10 chars) + space → col 31
#      - EXCEPT    ( 7 chars) + space → col 28
#      - UNION     ( 6 chars) + space → col 27
#    The keyword-agnostic shape demonstrates the gap is
#    in the set-op sub-grammar's tail (CORRESPONDING
#    isn't a recognized continuation), not in any per-
#    verb handler.  The BY (cols) form parses to the
#    same column index (the parser bails at
#    CORRESPONDING before it sees BY), so even the
#    explicit-column-match form of the SQL-standard
#    clause is unreachable.
#
# B. The ALTER SCHEMA family.  This one pins a notable
#    TWO-LAYER REJECTION SPLIT:
#
#      Layer 1 — sqlparser-rs accepts the statement,
#      convert.rs rejects it as
#      "unsupported statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement":
#        - ALTER SCHEMA sch1 RENAME TO sch2
#        - ALTER SCHEMA sch1 OWNER TO postgres
#        - ALTER SCHEMA IF EXISTS sch1 RENAME TO sch2
#      sqlparser-rs has AST nodes for these, but the
#      conversion layer doesn't dispatch to a handler
#      so they uniformly fall through to the generic
#      AST-level reject fingerprint
#      ("unsupported statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement").
#
#      Layer 2 — sqlparser-rs rejects before convert.rs
#      ever sees the AST, with
#      "Expected: ALTER SCHEMA operation, found: <KW>
#      at Line: 1, Column: 19" (col 19 = position right
#      after "ALTER SCHEMA sch1 ", i.e. where the
#      operation keyword starts):
#        - ALTER SCHEMA sch1 OWNED BY postgres
#        - ALTER SCHEMA sch1 SET SEARCH_PATH = 'public'
#      These operations aren't in sqlparser-rs's ALTER
#      SCHEMA sub-grammar at all, so they parse-reject
#      with a positional error instead of the generic
#      AST-level reject.
#
#    The split is informative for app porters: the
#    Layer-1 forms have a recognizable AST and could in
#    principle be wired up later with no grammar
#    change; the Layer-2 forms need a sqlparser-rs
#    grammar extension first.  Both layers surface as
#    errno 1064 over the MySQL wire so the surface-
#    level diagnostic is uniform; the split is only
#    visible via the message body.
#
# Working CREATE SCHEMA baseline — `CREATE SCHEMA sch1`
# works (no echo output, just the statement-echo
# line).  Confirms the SCHEMA noun isn't itself the
# gap — only the ALTER verb on it is.  The DROP
# SCHEMA at teardown also works, confirming DROP is
# wired even though ALTER isn't.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   The SQL-standard `CORRESPONDING [BY (cols)]` set-
#   op column-matching clause is entirely unreachable
#   on zeta across all three set-op verbs (UNION,
#   INTERSECT, EXCEPT) and both forms (bare and
#   explicit-by-cols).  Apps emitting standard SQL
#   that uses CORRESPONDING for name-based column
#   alignment (instead of position-based) must rewrite
#   each branch to project columns in the same
#   positional order — a mechanical transformation but
#   one that requires knowing both sides' column
#   names.
#
#   ALTER SCHEMA is similarly unreachable for every
#   shape probed (RENAME TO, OWNER TO, IF EXISTS
#   RENAME, OWNED BY, SET SEARCH_PATH).  The CREATE
#   and DROP halves of the schema lifecycle work, but
#   schemas are immutable after creation — there is no
#   in-place rename or ownership transfer.  The
#   workaround is to create a new schema, recreate the
#   contents inside it, and drop the old one.  This
#   makes any tool that performs schema-level
#   refactors (e.g. ownership normalization scripts,
#   tenant-renaming jobs, multi-schema migration tools)
#   non-portable until either the grammar (Layer 2) or
#   convert.rs (Layer 1) gains support.
#
# Wire divergence check: the existing UNION
# CORRESPONDING probe (set_ops_edges_basic_extra:111)
# runs on the mysqlwire path.  The grammar lives in
# sqlparser-rs which is shared by both wires, so
# pgwire is expected to give the same fingerprint.
# ALTER SCHEMA likewise routes through convert.rs in
# both wires.  (Not separately probed here — the
# shared-grammar / shared-converter design means the
# fingerprint is wire-agnostic at this layer.)
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — INTERSECT CORRESPONDING
#     (bare) parse-rejects with "Expected: SELECT,
#     VALUES, or a subquery in the query body, found:
#     CORRESPONDING at Line: 1, Column: 31".  Workaround:
#     project columns positionally in matching order on
#     both sides.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — INTERSECT CORRESPONDING BY
#     (cols) parse-rejects at the same column (parser
#     bails at CORRESPONDING before reading BY).  Same
#     workaround.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — EXCEPT CORRESPONDING parse-
#     rejects at col 28 (shorter verb keyword shifts the
#     position).  Same workaround.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — EXCEPT CORRESPONDING BY
#     (cols) parse-rejects at col 28.  Same workaround.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — UNION CORRESPONDING BY
#     (cols) parse-rejects at col 27 (extends the
#     existing bare-UNION-CORRESPONDING coverage with
#     the BY variant).  Same workaround.
#   # zeta-adapt: BASELINE — CREATE SCHEMA sch1 works.
#     DROP SCHEMA sch1 also works.  The lifecycle has
#     no ALTER verb.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — Layer 1 (AST-level reject):
#     ALTER SCHEMA ... RENAME TO / OWNER TO / IF EXISTS
#     RENAME TO all give "unsupported statement:
#     sqlparser::ast::Statement".  sqlparser-rs has the
#     AST nodes; the convert.rs dispatch is missing.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — Layer 2 (grammar-level
#     reject): ALTER SCHEMA ... OWNED BY / SET <param>
#     give "Expected: ALTER SCHEMA operation, found:
#     <KW> at Line: 1, Column: 19".  sqlparser-rs's
#     ALTER SCHEMA sub-grammar doesn't include these
#     operations.  Workaround for any ALTER SCHEMA
#     shape: create-new-and-migrate.

--echo === Setup ===

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS ca;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS cb;
CREATE TABLE ca (a INT, b INT);
CREATE TABLE cb (b INT, c INT);
INSERT INTO ca VALUES (1, 2), (3, 4);
INSERT INTO cb VALUES (2, 5), (4, 6);

--echo === INTERSECT CORRESPONDING (bare) — "found: CORRESPONDING at Line: 1, Column: 31" ===

# sqlparser-rs set-op sub-grammar terminates at the
# verb keyword; CORRESPONDING is not recognized as a
# continuation.  Column 31 = position right after the
# space following INTERSECT in the prefix
# "SELECT a, b FROM ca INTERSECT ".
--error 1064
SELECT a, b FROM ca INTERSECT CORRESPONDING SELECT b, c FROM cb;

--echo === INTERSECT CORRESPONDING BY (b) — same fingerprint, same col (parser bails at CORRESPONDING before reading BY) ===

--error 1064
SELECT a, b FROM ca INTERSECT CORRESPONDING BY (b) SELECT b, c FROM cb;

--echo === EXCEPT CORRESPONDING (bare) — "found: CORRESPONDING at Line: 1, Column: 28" (shorter verb keyword shifts the position) ===

--error 1064
SELECT a, b FROM ca EXCEPT CORRESPONDING SELECT b, c FROM cb;

--echo === EXCEPT CORRESPONDING BY (b) — same fingerprint, same col ===

--error 1064
SELECT a, b FROM ca EXCEPT CORRESPONDING BY (b) SELECT b, c FROM cb;

--echo === UNION CORRESPONDING BY (b) — "found: CORRESPONDING at Line: 1, Column: 27" (extends existing bare-UNION coverage with the BY variant) ===

# The bare UNION CORRESPONDING probe lives in
# set_ops_edges_basic_extra:111.  This probe extends
# that to the BY (cols) variant.
--error 1064
SELECT a, b FROM ca UNION CORRESPONDING BY (b) SELECT b, c FROM cb;

--echo === BASELINE (works): CREATE SCHEMA — confirms the SCHEMA noun is wired; only the ALTER verb is the gap ===

DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS sch1;
CREATE SCHEMA sch1;

--echo === ALTER SCHEMA RENAME TO — Layer 1 (AST-level reject): "unsupported statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement" ===

# sqlparser-rs accepts this and produces an AST node;
# convert.rs has no dispatch for ALTER SCHEMA so it
# falls through to the generic unsupported-statement
# message.
--error 1064
ALTER SCHEMA sch1 RENAME TO sch2;

--echo === ALTER SCHEMA OWNER TO — Layer 1 (AST-level reject): same fingerprint as RENAME TO ===

--error 1064
ALTER SCHEMA sch1 OWNER TO postgres;

--echo === ALTER SCHEMA IF EXISTS ... RENAME TO — Layer 1 (AST-level reject): IF EXISTS guard doesn't change the layer ===

# Even with the IF EXISTS guard (which would normally
# turn a missing-target error into a silent no-op),
# the convert.rs dispatch still rejects.  The reject
# fires before the IF EXISTS check would have been
# evaluated.
--error 1064
ALTER SCHEMA IF EXISTS sch1 RENAME TO sch2;

--echo === ALTER SCHEMA ... OWNED BY — Layer 2 (grammar-level reject): "Expected: ALTER SCHEMA operation, found: OWNED at Line: 1, Column: 19" ===

# sqlparser-rs's ALTER SCHEMA sub-grammar doesn't
# include OWNED BY as a recognized operation, so it
# rejects with a positional error before convert.rs
# ever sees an AST.  Column 19 = position right after
# "ALTER SCHEMA sch1 " (18 chars + 1).
--error 1064
ALTER SCHEMA sch1 OWNED BY postgres;

--echo === ALTER SCHEMA ... SET SEARCH_PATH — Layer 2 (grammar-level reject): "Expected: ALTER SCHEMA operation, found: SET at Line: 1, Column: 19" ===

# Same Layer 2 fingerprint, different keyword token.
# Confirms the rejection is keyword-agnostic at the
# ALTER SCHEMA operation slot.
--error 1064
ALTER SCHEMA sch1 SET SEARCH_PATH = 'public';

DROP SCHEMA sch1;
DROP TABLE ca;
DROP TABLE cb;
